A||N||14||Monkeyman||What do I need for a 3.2??||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||10-20-2000||05:31 AM||||Here's the scenario.  I'm on my way to getting a little more power in the Grape Ape.  I don't have much to spend so I'll probably just get my current motor rebuilt.  My local mechanic generally does "stock" only, but about 6 months ago hinted that he would do a 3.2/3.2 rebuild for me.<P>Since I have a limited budget, I need to know what I (he) can and can't do with the motor.  I'd love to go all out and get exotic with special heads and pistons, etc, but I know that stuff is expensive.  How about some suggestions on what parts I (he) will need to:  One, rebuild my 2.8 into either a 3.1 or a 3.2.  And, two, what (if any) performance mods he can do (cam?) to squeeze a little more power out of it in the process.  He has the ability/equipment to do a good valve job so that's not an issue.  Other than a 3.1 crankshaft, what other parts will he need just to convert it to a 3.1?  What cam should he use?  Any certain pistons, etc that would help in the power department and still be reasonably cheap and reliable?<P>My mechanic knows what he's doing, but since he usually only does "stock", any suggestions would improve my chances for success.<P>Thanx, all.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||5
Z||000001||Old Lar||10-20-2000||07:14 AM||||Pisa, FOCOA and others offer a 3.1 rebuild kit for $350.  Add labor costs, then figure out what else you have money for.  Your mechanic should be able to tell you what can or can't be done and you know what your budget will let you do.<P>When he does the rebuild, expect to find more things that will need to be replaced or repaired, which will eat into your budget.<P>What ever your budget is, double it.||||reg||10
Z||000002||Phil||10-20-2000||08:10 AM||||Lar<BR>I belive Pisa's 2.8 to 3.1 conversion kit is closer to $1000- crank, pistons, adjust. fuel reg. and flywheel/flex plate. I had a quote for a rebuild (needed crank, pistons and cam anyway) and he charged me only $75 extra to convert to a 3.1 plus a flywheel. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Shaun41178||10-20-2000||01:56 PM||||I have  parts list of all the major components I used in my 3.1 buildup with part numbers and cost.  The only things different from a 2.8-3.1 is the crank and the pistons.  Everything else can be purchased for use for a 2.8 like gaskets and timing chain and things like that.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178</A> <P>Sorry I couldn't give a working link but copying and pasting always works for me.<P>Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||10-20-2000||08:15 PM||||Old Lar--I don't just want a bigger motor.  It really needs to be rebuilt so I'm counting on the mechanic to plan for whatever parts are normally necessary for a rebuild.<P>Apparently we have a slight disagreement in what is required to change a 2.8 into a 3.2.  One says only the crank and pistons are different, another says fuel regulator and flywheel, too.  I would imagine that nothing major would need to be done to my motor during the rebuild as it's currently running.  If it comes down to it, I'll just have it rebuilt to stock specs and start budgeting for something larger/more powerful in a few years.  At least it will be reliable.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phil||10-20-2000||08:36 PM||||If you have a pre 88 2.8 it is externally balanced (weights on the flywheel) . The 3.1 is internally balanced(flywheel spins neutral). Hence the possible need for a flywheel||||reg||1
Z||000006||Monkeyman||10-20-2000||08:43 PM||||Phil--My motor is out of an '87 so it must be externally balanced.  Do you think I'd still need a different flywheel even if I was just boring and stroking my stock 2.8?  What's the price range of a flywheel?||||reg||1
Z||000007||N_Man||10-20-2000||09:05 PM||||Most machinists can shave the counterbalance weight off you current flywheel.  Then get just the flywheel balcaced before you get everything balanced.<P>If possible get a rear drive 3.1 crank because(i believe) they didn't use DIS which will save alot of weight.||||reg||1
Z||000008||BigBoyToys||10-20-2000||09:36 PM||||Monkeywhatever,<BR>Simply put to change an engine from one displacement to another (2.8litre to a 3.xlitre) displacement means each cylinder must end up holding more. Notice if you divide 2.8 by 6 (the number of cylinders) you get .46 litres which is actually what each cylinder (when piston is at the bottom of it's stroke)will hold in volume/litre (approximately 1/2 liter of soda). <P>What GM did was to take the same block and get more (.05 litres per cylinder to get a 3.1) displacement in each cylinder was to squat (shorten) the piston height and increase the stroke (pull the piston down a little bit more by changing the crank. The piston connects to the connecting rod, the connecting rod connects to the crank, thus connecting the piston to the crank. The new crank is about the same (except balancing) with the exception that each point where the connecting rod attaches to the crank is now fruther from the centerline of the crank which will now pull the piston down inside the cylinder that little bit more needed to gain the extra displacement. Some may think why not just use shorter connecting rods but then clearances to the piston inner wall and connecting rod would interfere.<P>For the 2.8/3.1 block to get to a 3.2 the cylinders need to be bored larger (meaning the diameter increases) only enough to add an accumalative displacement of .1 litres (or .016 litres per cylinder). Typically when you bore the cylinders it means larger diameter pistons so the gap between the cylinder wall and the side of the piston is not to large.<P>So much for the 101 talk and hope this helps in understanding what is done to get from a 2.8 to a 3.2. There are a lot of posts about this exact thing and it seems that the most bang for the buck ends up being the 3.4 short block upgrade. You keep the top end and get more torque and a more robust block imo.<P>BBTs||||reg||3
Z||000009||88formula||10-20-2000||09:51 PM||||Use the GM 12363220 camshaft with your 3.1 rebuild. I have tested lots of camshafts for the 2.8, 3.1, and 3.4-liter engines and the GM camshaft makes the most power with very good torque. It is the same camshaft that is used in the 3.4-liter s-10 conversion motor that GM sells in their performance catalog.<P>The Crane 2030 is also a good choice but it favors the bottom end a little more than the GM camshaft does. Both camshafts are excellent choices.<P>3.1 liter engine with 8.8 compression, ported manifolds, stock heads, and a .030" overbore.<P>The GM camshaft makes 163 HP @ 5000 rpm and 199 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm.<P>The Crane camshaft makes 163 HP @ 5000 rpm and 202 ft. lbs. of torque @ 3500 rpm. <P>These are peak numbers. This simulation was done on Dyno2000.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Shaun41178||10-21-2000||03:21 AM||||Ok I did leave one thing out and that is the flexplate/flywheel.<P>Monkeyman you have an auto or manual?<P>When you get a crank it will most likely be from a FWD car as they are more popular.  SO yes you will need a 3.1 crankshaft.  One can be bought from GM or a remanufactured one can be supplied to you.  You will need pistons for an iron head 3.1.  You will need a flexplate/flywheel for the 3.1 crank.  If you get a crank from a FWD car then you need a neutrally balanced flexplate.  Don't worry about not finding one.  They are everywhere.  You might even get one with the crank depending on what kind of arraingment you work out.  However if you have an autozone or Pep boys or anything like that you can get a new Flexplate or flywheel from them.  A flexplate for a 3.1 FWD crank cost me like $38 from Autozone.  I still had to have it balanced with the reciprocating assembly.<P>I would ask your machinist if it would be possible to balance the assembly with the neutral 3.1 crank and the 2.8 flex/fly.  He might be able to do that without cutting the piece off of the 2.8 flex/fly.  It might be possible for him to take metal out of the crank to balance it or add a heavier metal.  If he has to add a heavy metal to balance it then it could be costly.  I would just recommend a new flex/fly from autozone or your local GM whichever is cheaper.  See what your machinist recommends.<P>And you don't need an adj fuel pressure regulator!!!!!!  Don't let anyone tell you different.  The stock one will work fine.  If you want to fine tune the fuel pressure for maximum performance then get a adj one but make sure you have a dyno nearby to test it on cause by making an adjustment the wrong way you could actually be hurting performance.<P>pistons on my website are listed with price.  I am told these are hyperhuetectic pistons which are stronger then stockers but not much more expensive<P>A 3.1 is not hard to do.  Don't let it intimidate you.  If you want just buy the same parts I got and you will be ok.  And get the cam that 88Formula recommends.  I bought that cam too but I am also using 1.6 rockers which puts lift at .455 and .484 respectively.  With small other mods I have I expect about 200 hp from my setup.  Mostly head work  <P>All parts can be viewed on my website for a 3.1 <A HREF="http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178" TARGET=_blank>http://members.aol.com/Shaun41178</A> <P>------------------<BR>Shaun-Toledo OH, 85 GT<BR>3.2 With nitrous<BR>http://members.aol.com/shaun41178||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||10-21-2000||05:14 AM||||BigBoyToys--I understood the basics, just not the specifics, but thanks.<P>88formula--Got prices on the cams?  I thought most cams were somewhere around the $500 mark but (hopefully) I'm wrong.<P>Shaun--For $40, I'll just have him buy a new flywheel/flexplate.  Probably cheaper than doing a bunch of machine work.<P>All--I wish I was able to do the work myself.  If I could, I'm sure I could get a much stronger motor for well under my budget.  Unfortunately, I'm probably the most mechanically challenged of this bunch.  Even if I tried it myself, I'd probably spend 6 months trying to get it right and the mechanic can do it in a week.  The Grape Ape is a daily driver so time is valuable.  Keep the suggestions coming.  I want to be able to bring a list of parts and services I need to the table when I talk to the mechanic.  Like I said before, he's very much into "stock" so he will probably be much more receptive to this if I can provide part numbers, etc..<P>Thanx for the help <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">.||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||10-21-2000||05:37 PM||||$200 for the GM camshaft with lifters. <A HREF="http://www.spoperformanceparts.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.spoperformanceparts.com/</A>  <BR>$160 for the Crane camshaft with lifters. <A HREF="http://www.jegs.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jegs.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 10-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||10-21-2000||09:17 PM||||I think we finally got most of the info down here, but to repeat... 1) you need a 3.1 crank and because the longer stroke crank is balanced differently, you also need... 2) the flywheel/flexplate that goes with the 3.1 crank in order to get the correct balance, 3) I think you need the 3.1 harmonic balancer too (not sure on that), 4) pistons for the 3.1 crank (the wrist pin is located in a different place). As was pointed out, you want the pistons designed for use with cast iron heads (like an early 90's front wheel drive GM minivan). Heck, find one of those engines real cheap and you have all the parts you need. Reread Ed Parks article about engine swaps...good info.<P>Yes, a good machine shop can modify the old flywheel, if they know what they're doing and if they are good. Since there are a lot of "ifs" and those 3.1 parts are cheap and laying around in boneyards everywhere, I'm not sure I'd mess with machining stuff to fit. It's real easy to screw this stuff up.<P>MM, get the GM hipo cam, it's used in lots of "stock" engines, noteably, the 3.4 performance crate engines. Just tell your mechanic to use "this stock cam" and give him the part number. Rest assured that this cam was designed to work with computer controlled cars. As far as the Crane CompuCams, there are actually two for the 2.8/3.1/3.4 engines. One is made for automatics and another one with a little more duration is for manual shift cars.<P>I'm sure he's had kids in the past wanting him to put a big cam in their engines and then either wasn't happy because it killed all their torque, won't work right with the ECM, or worse, they ran the piss out of the engine because it was hopped up and when it was toast, came back to him and tried claiming it didn't last because the mechanic did something wrong.||||reg||1
Z||000014||BOILERMAKER||10-21-2000||09:35 PM||||If memory serves me correctly, Monkeyman, a flywheel won't do you much good since you really need a flexplate (auto).  From what I've heard, you still need the 88 flexplate also (haven't done the auto, so don't know for sure).  I've done it (the 2.8 conversion) first hand (along with many other forum members here) and would be more than happy to let you in on what I did.<p>[This message has been edited by BOILERMAKER (edited 10-22-2000).]||||reg||1
